WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780 line:17%
A hearing aid can change how someone hears, and

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.940 line:17%
it can change how other people react.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.570 line:17%
Imagine a classmate sees an aid and starts answering

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:09.770 line:17%
for the student who wears it.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.770 line:17%
That assumption is an example of the hearing aid

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:13.410 line:17%
effect.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.330 line:17%
If the student repeatedly gets that message, it can

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.170 line:17%
shape how they see their own abilities.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.570 line:17%
A better response starts by asking the student what

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.590 line:17%
would help them participate.

00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:26.873 line:17%
Now picture two adults talking about a disappointing day.

00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:30.233 line:17%
A child who misses that conversation loses a chance

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:32.573 line:17%
to hear a feeling named and a coping strategy

00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:33.213 line:17%
explained.

00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:36.533 line:17%
An adult can bring that language within reach during

00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:37.453 line:17%
the next exchange.

00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:40.633 line:17%
They might say, I felt disappointed when our plans

00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:42.373 line:17%
changed, and I needed a minute to think.

00:00:43.323 --> 00:00:46.163 line:17%
Words for feelings help children explain their own experiences

00:00:46.163 --> 00:00:47.963 line:17%
and understand how someone else feels.

00:00:48.723 --> 00:00:50.903 line:17%
Those skills make it easier to work through a

00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:52.063 line:17%
disagreement with a friend.
